.TH USB\-WAKEUP\-BLOCKER 1 "2026-08-30" "usb-wakeup-blocker 1.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
usb\-wakeup\-blocker \- control which USB devices may wake the system from sleep
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B usb\-wakeup\-blocker
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B usb\-wakeup\-blocker
disables USB remote wakeup for the devices you choose, so that a nudged mouse
or a stray USB signal no longer brings the machine out of sleep. By default it
blocks only mice; keyboards and every other device keep their ability to wake
the system.
.PP
On Linux the tool reads and writes the
.I power/wakeup
attribute of each device under
.IR /sys/bus/usb/devices ,
falling back to
.BR lsusb (8)
only to classify devices and to fill in missing product names. On macOS it
drives an IOKit helper instead.
.PP
Applying changes requires root privileges. Read-only modes such as
.B \-l
still need root to read every device attribute.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Block all USB devices from waking the system.
.TP
.B \-m
Block only mice. This is the default.
.TP
.B \-c
Block both mice and keyboards.
.TP
.BI \-w " PRODUCT"
Whitelist a USB device by product name, so it keeps its ability to wake the
system. May be given more than once. Use the
.B Product
field shown by
.B \-v
or
.B \-l
to find the right name; a partial match is enough.
.TP
.B \-d
Dry run. Show what would change without touching any device.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose output, including a table of every device and its state.
.TP
.B \-l
List the current wakeup status of all USB devices and exit without changing
anything.
.TP
.BI \-p " PATH"
Act on one device only, given as a sysfs path on Linux or a location ID on
macOS. This is the form the udev rule uses for hot-plugged devices.
.TP
.B \-\-daemon
macOS only. Run as a persistent daemon that disables wakeup before each sleep.
.TP
.B \-h
Show a usage summary and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/usb-wakeup-blocker.conf
Options passed to the systemd service, as
.BR ARGS .
Preserved across package upgrades.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/usb-wakeup-blocker.service
One-shot unit that applies the policy at boot.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/99-usb-wakeup-blocker.rules
Applies the policy to devices plugged in while the system is running.
.SH EXAMPLES
Block mice and keyboards, but let one keyboard through:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
usb\-wakeup\-blocker \-c \-w "My Keyboard"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Block everything except a wireless receiver:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
usb\-wakeup\-blocker \-a \-w "USB Receiver"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the current state without changing anything:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
usb\-wakeup\-blocker \-l
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success and a non-zero value if the arguments are invalid or the
device state could not be read or written.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lsusb (8),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR udev (7)
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at <https://github.com/nogunix/usb-wakeup-blocker/issues>.
